Independent std-3 review evidence, commit d3975a0

criteria.md was written before reading std-3.md.
All new source, tools and outputs live here; no tracked file was edited.

Numerical checks:
  venv/bin/python math_review.py       # 254 comparisons
  venv/bin/python extra_math.py        # 59 comparisons, includes midpoint search
  venv/bin/python hypot_ties.py        # six realizable exact midpoint ties
  venv/bin/python underflow_review.py  # 12 comparisons, including atan2(minsub,2)
Total: 331 comparisons. All passed. MPFR 4.2.2, directed 4096-bit rounding.
The model uses exact GMP rationals for the note's algorithm. MPFR is an
independent oracle; its directed endpoints must both round to the same binary64.
These are not tests of an implemented std/float extension. No cross-engine
execution was performed.

Plain-i probes (from the review worktree):
  direnv exec /home/agent/Programming/i /tmp/review-std3/i test --project /tmp/review-std3/bench pbt_test --time
  direnv exec /home/agent/Programming/i /tmp/review-std3/i test --project /tmp/review-std3/bench reduced_ordinary --time
  direnv exec /home/agent/Programming/i /tmp/review-std3/i test --project /tmp/review-std3/bench reduced_ordinary --time --opt 2
  direnv exec /home/agent/Programming/i timeout 60 /tmp/review-std3/i test --project /tmp/review-std3/bench reduced_huge --time
  direnv exec /home/agent/Programming/i /tmp/review-std3/i test --project /tmp/review-std3/bench fixed_test --time
  direnv exec /home/agent/Programming/i /tmp/review-std3/i test --project /tmp/review-std3/failures

The mathematical probes originally used the shared heavy wrapper before direnv.
No cargo commands were run. i is a copy of the integration lane's cached binary;
its hash is in metadata.json and matches the binary reported by the note. A
build-to-source commit link is not established. Thus timings describe this
available compiler/runtime, not a fresh build independently traced to a commit.
The review commit differs from the note's source baseline only in std-3.md.

Results:
- Exact rational reduced sine kernel for input 1.0, 15 terms: 39.839 seconds
  O0 / 40.972 seconds O2 (CPU 39.26 / 40.31 seconds), excluding compilation.
  Reduced midpoint denominator: 331 bits; intermediate rationals about 9,679 bits.
- The corresponding rational sine kernel for input 1e300 did not finish within
  a 60-second process timeout (exit 124, includes compilation). Midpoint
  denominator: 2,916 bits; model intermediates about 84,626 bits.
- atan(1/5), 250 exact terms: 2.043 seconds, CPU 2.01 seconds (initial run).
- Fixed-point 128-bit outward cosine enclosure for the reduced input of sin(1):
  0.001 seconds O0 / 0.002 seconds O2. Both endpoints round to sin(1)'s correct
  bits, 0x3feaed548f090cee. This is a one-input kernel feasibility probe, not
  a full implementation or a performance bound. Its remainder allowance is one
  fixed-point unit, exceeding 1/40! for the 20 cosine terms on |r|<1.
- pbt: five tests pass; partial P2/P4/P7 kernels, not the proposed full API.
  Includes 20 seeds with depth-four dependent trees, prescribed greedy path,
  zero-budget thunk avoidance, cyclic-tree budget, and a mapped 90 -> 100 case.
- failures: one ordinary property assertion failure, three nonfinite-bit stops,
  and one passing test catching the adapter's final assertion. Exit 1 as expected.

The rational kernel already cross-cancels products and denominator gcds in sums.
Its cost is evidence against accepting the current baseline without a feasibility
milestone, not a lower bound for every possible exact-rational implementation.
Only fixed_test and pbt_test should be run together casually; the unfiltered
benchmark suite contains the intentionally expensive rational tests.
